#!/bin/bash

# 50-xdg-portal-restart - restart the desktop portals after resuming
#
# systemd-sleep runs this as root with two arguments:
#   $1  pre|post                          (post is the resume)
#   $2  suspend|hibernate|hybrid-sleep|suspend-then-hibernate
#
# Why a resume hook is needed on top of Restart=: suspending does not stop a
# process, it stops time for it. A portal backend wakes up still holding what it
# had before - a Wayland connection, a pty to a terminal, a GPU context - and
# some of that does not survive the trip. The process is then alive but useless:
# on the bus, answering method calls, never drawing a window, so the next Save
# dialog in any app hangs or comes back "cancelled" with nothing on screen to
# explain it. systemd cannot see any of this, because the service never exited
# and Restart= only ever fires on an exit.
#
# A process started after the resume has nothing stale to reuse, so the cheapest
# reliable answer is to discard the ones from before it. /usr/local/bin/
# xdg-portal-restart already restarts the portals in every logged-in session and
# is reused here rather than reimplemented, so there is a single description of
# how a session's portals get restarted (the pacman hook is the other caller).
#
# Only post acts. The pre half is the wrong moment to be stopping services, and
# a session that could not be reached then would only hold up the suspend.
#
# The helper exits 0 even when a session cannot be reached, so a resume is never
# blocked by a portal problem; what it could not do it reports on stderr, which
# systemd-sleep forwards to the journal.

set -uo pipefail

[ "${1:-}" = post ] || exit 0

exec /usr/local/bin/xdg-portal-restart
